cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "CSF-Bench: Causal Self-Forecasting Lab"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Ortiz
    given-names: Jacob
    alias: agentjakey
repository-code: "https://github.com/agentjakey/causal-self-forecasting"
abstract: >-
  Research code for a preregistered external state-information audit on Gemma 3 1B, asking
  whether access to a model's correct prompt-specific hidden state improves forecasts of how a
  fixed internal intervention changes its clean preferred answer, beyond the prompt, the clean
  output distribution, and a complete numerical description of the intervention. Predictors are
  ridge regressions fitted and controlled by the authors; a ridge regression reading a residual
  stream is a readout, not a self-report. Includes a frozen prompt split, a constructed
  direction family, preregistered intervention-strength calibration, a matched wrong-state
  control, and a salted-hash precommitment protocol. The contribution is primarily the benchmark;
  the first application returned a null. Both preregistered comparisons produced 95 percent paired
  bootstrap intervals that cross zero, and the lowest point-estimate error came from the
  intervention encoding alone. Training and final test shared a fixed family of 16 signed
  interventions, so no method was asked to generalize to an unseen intervention. This is not
  evidence that hidden states carry no information about intervention effects, and it says nothing
  about introspection or about larger models.
keywords:
  - interpretability
  - ai-safety
  - causal-intervention
  - benchmark
  - language-models
